---
/** Mobile only. On a phone the page is 1,700 words and the only CTAs were at the
    very top and very bottom.

    The second button used to be "Free consultation" pointing at #consultation —
    an id that only exists on the band that renders the lead form, which only the
    homepage had. On the other four pages it scrolled nowhere. `quoteHref` is
    resolved per page: the in-page form if there is one, the contact page if not.

    `tagline` is optional and comes from config rather than being written here.
    This engine serves other sites, and a strapline naming three services is a
    claim, not decoration — it has to be false-able per site, and absent by
    default. */
const { phone, phoneHref, quoteHref, hasChat = false, tagline = null } = Astro.props;
---
<div class:list={['sticky', hasChat && 'sticky--chat']}>
  {tagline && <p class="sticky__tag">{tagline}</p>}
  <div class="sticky__row">
    <a class="btn sticky__btn" href={phoneHref}>Call {phone}</a>
    <a class="btn btn--ghost sticky__btn" href={quoteHref}>Get a quote</a>
  </div>
</div>
<style>
  .sticky { display: none; }
  @media (max-width: 700px) {
    .sticky { display: block;
      position: fixed; inset: auto 0 0 0; z-index: 40; padding: 0.6rem 0.9rem;
      background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--bg) 94%, transparent);
      backdrop-filter: blur(8px); border-top: 1px solid var(--border); }
    .sticky__row { display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr; gap: 0.5rem; }
    .sticky__btn { justify-content: center; padding-block: 0.75rem; }

    /* The strapline sits ABOVE the button row, which is what lets it use the
       full width: the bubble is pinned low enough that only the row has to give
       up space for it. Below the buttons would have been the obvious place —
       there is a 25px gap there — but that strip is inside the home-indicator
       zone on every modern iPhone, so anything readable put there gets crossed
       by it.

       Set as an eyebrow rather than a sentence, matching how the rest of the
       site labels a band, and in --bronze-ink rather than --bronze: the plain
       bronze is a fill and is documented as not carrying text contrast.

       Ellipsis rather than wrap. A second line would push the button row up and
       break its alignment with the bubble, so a tagline too long for the bar
       should truncate visibly instead of silently costing the layout. */
    .sticky__tag {
      margin: 0 0 0.4rem; line-height: 1.3;
      font-family: var(--font-ui); font-size: var(--step--2); font-weight: 600;
      letter-spacing: 0.09em; text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--bronze-ink);
      white-space: nowrap; overflow: hidden; text-overflow: ellipsis;
    }

    /* Both values below exist to lower the Call button's MIN-CONTENT width, not
     * to restyle it. The columns are 1fr 1fr, and `1fr` is `minmax(auto, 1fr)` —
     * a column cannot shrink below its content, so with `nowrap` the long "Call
     * <number>" label pushes its own column wider and takes the difference out
     * of "Get a quote". That is what made the two buttons visibly different
     * sizes. Keeping the content inside half the bar is what keeps them equal.
     *
     * Shrinking the inline padding costs nothing to look at: the labels are
     * centred, so once a button is wider than its text the padding is slack. It
     * only ever mattered as the floor.
     *
     * `nowrap` is the guard, not the fix. Without it the label wraps to two
     * lines rather than overflowing, which leaves the Call button taller than
     * its neighbour while every width measurement still reports the text as
     * fitting. It fits by wrapping. Forbid the wrap and a label that is
     * genuinely too long shows up as overflow, where it can be seen. */
    .sticky--chat .sticky__btn {
      padding-inline: 0.5rem; white-space: nowrap; font-size: var(--step--1);
    }

    /* Keep the button row out from under the chat bubble, horizontally and
     * vertically. Everything here is dictated by a third party.
     *
     * GHL pins its bubble bottom-right — 20px in, 58px across, 58px tall — and
     * gives no way to move it: no position custom property among the ~25 it
     * exposes, and no offset field in the platform (confirmed with Nick,
     * 2026-08-06). So the bar moves instead.
     *
     * Horizontally: the bubble's footprint is 78px from the viewport edge, and
     * clearing it with a small gap wants 5.4rem. This is on the ROW rather than
     * the bar, so the bar's background still spans full width and the strapline
     * above still gets all of it — but the bar already contributes 0.9rem of its
     * own right padding, so the row only has to make up the remaining 4.5rem.
     * Setting the full 5.4rem here double-counts that 0.9rem and takes 14.4px
     * off the row, which is enough to break the buttons' equal widths at 360px
     * and below.
     *
     * Vertically: the bubble's centre sits 49px up and does not move. The bar
     * hugged the bottom with 0.6rem of padding, putting the buttons' centre
     * around 34px — a visible 13px offset. 25px of bottom padding plus a fixed
     * 24px half-height lands on 49px exactly. Pinning the height matters as much
     * as the padding: the buttons ran 45.4px to 47.8px depending on viewport
     * because the type scale is fluid, so any padding chosen here would be
     * correct at one width only. 48px also stays clear of the 44px minimum tap
     * target.
     *
     * All of it applies only when a widget is configured — this engine serves
     * sites with no chat, and they should not pay a 5.4rem hole and 25px of
     * padding for a bubble that never renders. */
    .sticky--chat { padding-bottom: 25px; }
    .sticky--chat .sticky__row { padding-right: 4.5rem; }
    .sticky--chat .sticky__btn { block-size: 48px; padding-block: 0; }
  }

  /* Below ~350px the padding is already as low as it goes and the label still
   * will not fit in half the bar, so the type takes the last step. Measured at
   * 340px: the number needs ~103px against a ~98px budget.
   *
   * A tenth off is enough and is bounded — the column never has to grow, so the
   * buttons stay equal, which is the whole point of the rules above. */
  @media (max-width: 359px) {
    .sticky--chat .sticky__btn { font-size: calc(var(--step--1) * 0.9); }
  }
</style>
<style is:global>
  /* The bar is fixed, so it sits ON TOP of the end of the page and the footer
     has to be pushed clear of it. This used to be `.sticky + *`, which resolved
     to the ChatWidget's <script> tag — padding on a script does nothing, so the
     footer stayed underneath.

     Two reservations because the bar has two heights. The chat variant carries
     a strapline and 25px of bottom padding that the plain one does not; measured
     at 111px against the plain bar's 67px. `:has()` is the only way to reach the
     body from here, and its fallback is benign — a browser without it keeps the
     plain reservation, which is what every browser got before this line existed.

     Both values are measured, not derived. If the bar's parts change, re-measure
     rather than adjusting by eye: being short here does not look broken, it just
     quietly hides the last line of the footer. */
  @media (max-width: 700px) {
    body { padding-bottom: 4.5rem; }
    body:has(.sticky--chat) { padding-bottom: 7.2rem; }
  }
</style>
